{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "iterative is the shared-datapath baseline: one micro-rotation/cycle with a single adder/shift stage, so it should be the area-cheapest family in my set. It is also the natural reference for the power index. I keep the ranges tight around the accuracy floor (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) so the budget maps the area/accuracy trade-off rather than wasting evals on grossly over-accurate or under-accurate corners. data_width 14-16 and n_iter 12-14 bracket the point where accuracy_bits crosses 12; angle_guard/frac_guard 0-1 and both rounding modes cover the small accuracy levers without inflating the datapath."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 1, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations/cycle on a shared FSM, so it raises throughput per cycle at the cost of a wider (k-deep) datapath and a longer critical path. The spec's hard constraint is throughput_msps >= 32 and a 32-request burst that must clear within 0.5 us of a 1 us tick (sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5). A 1-result/cycle iterative design at modest Fmax may not clear 32 results in 0.5 us, so unrolled_k is the family that plausibly meets the batch/throughput constraints. I cap k at 4 (not 8) because very large k pushes the critical path (and thus Fmax) down and inflates area, which fights the min-area objective; k=2..4 is where the throughput gain is bought at acceptable area/Fmax cost. Same accuracy brackets as iterative so the two fronts are directly comparable on the accuracy axis."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "I am restricted to iterative and unrolled_k, so the whole front must come from these two. The spec has two hard constraints that shape the search: (1) max_abs_err <= 2^-12, which pins the accuracy region to data_width ~14-16 with n_iter ~12-14 (accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, identical across families); and (2) throughput_msps >= 32 plus the system batch constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 (32 results within 0.5 us of a 1 us tick). The min-area objective plus max-accuracy_bits objective means the interesting trade-off is: how much area to spend to (a) clear the 32-request burst fast enough and (b) push accuracy a little above the 12-bit floor. iterative is the area floor but its 1-result/cycle throughput may fail the 0.5 us batch bound at realistic Fmax; unrolled_k buys throughput per cycle with a wider datapath and is the family most likely to satisfy the batch/throughput constraints. I split the budget 50/50 so both fronts are mapped over the same accuracy brackets, letting the merged Pareto front show where unrolled_k's extra area is actually needed to meet the system constraints versus where iterative already suffices. Ranges are kept narrow (not the full registry) to focus the 100-eval budget on the feasible trade-off band rather than on corners that are obviously too small (fail accuracy) or too large (waste area). I will not claim infeasible yet: both families have plausible feasible points, so I explore first and only declare infeasible if the merged front shows no design meeting all constraints."
}

